WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:08.000
Recruitment and Propaganda Plan You want to end war and disease but don't have a nonprofit, a bank account, or anything

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.200
to offer anyone. Here's how to recruit one hundred thousand humans before you have anything except an argument.

3
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:22.130
It's a Kickstarter where the reward tier is 'possibly not dying,' which is the most compelling crowdfunding offer in history.

4
00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:30.800
Phase zero: Collecting Humans Before You Have Anything to Offer Them On Wishonia, if someone discovered everyone was dying

5
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.440
from preventable causes, they would simply stop dying from preventable causes.

6
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:42.800
On Earth, you need a tax-exempt entity, a bank account, and written permission from something called the Securities and

7
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:50.060
Exchange Commission before you're allowed to try. The Securities and Exchange Commission regulates securities, which are

8
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:54.120
financial instruments, not the kind of security where people stop dying.

9
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.880
That would be a different department. You don't have that department.

10
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:05.900
So you can't accept money yet. You can't sell bonds yet. You can't do anything that involves the movement of small pieces

11
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:15.200
of paper. What you CAN do is collect names. The goal of Phase zero is building a database of every human who agrees that

12
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.820
dying from preventable causes is a strange thing to tolerate.

13
00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:27.400
It's a Kickstarter where the reward tier is "possibly not dying," which is the most compelling crowdfunding offer in history,

14
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:33.260
and yet somehow requires marketing. The Bureaucratic Nesting Doll Problem.

15
00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.840
On your planet, before you can accept donations, you need five-oh-one c three status.

16
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:45.400
Before you can sell bonds, you need S.E.C. registration. Before you can do either, you need a bank account.

17
00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.840
Before you can open a bank account, you need an organization.

18
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.940
Before you can have an organization, you need the bank account.

19
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:59.220
It's like a door that requires a key that's locked inside the room the door opens to.

20
00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:08.580
Your species built this system on purpose. The solution is the same one humans have used every time this has happened, which

21
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:16.000
is every time. You collect people first, then point at the crowd when the institutions ask "but who wants this?".

22
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.200
Abolition started with a list of names. Suffrage started with a list of names.

23
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:26.400
Civil rights started with a list of names. Someone wrote down who agreed.

24
00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.060
The names became a number. The number became bargaining power.

25
00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:37.460
The bargaining power became policy. Then the policy became a holiday, and everyone pretended they'd agreed all along.

26
00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:43.360
Your species does this every forty years and never recognizes the pattern.

27
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:51.660
Three Types of Humans Who Want to Help. People who agree with "dying is bad" fall into three categories.

28
00:02:52.460 --> 00:03:00.240
Spreadsheet Humans (Investors). These are humans who hear "two hundred and seventy-two percent annual return on Victory

29
00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:05.420
Bonds backed by redirected military spending" and immediately open a spreadsheet.

30
00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.960
Their pupils dilate. Their pulse quickens. They ask about the term structure.

31
00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:17.200
Then they ask about the term structure again. Then they ask a third question, which is also about the term structure.

32
00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:23.940
These humans are not broken. They are simply running financial software on moral hardware.

33
00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:30.360
They process "millions of people are dying" faster when it arrives formatted as a revenue projection.

34
00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:37.320
They'll wait years for a good investment to materialize. They will not wait thirty seconds for a bad one.

35
00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:44.880
If your math is solid, these humans will follow you to the gates of hell, provided the risk-adjusted return on entering

36
00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:50.620
hell is competitive. Do not try to make them cry. Make them calculate.

37
00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:57.450
Crying is temporary. Compound interest is forever. Heart Humans (Advocates).

38
00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:06.260
These are humans motivated by the mission itself. They share things on social media, write letters to politicians, volunteer

39
00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:11.060
time, donate money they probably shouldn't, and cry at commercials about dogs.

40
00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:16.279
They are, frankly, the only reason your species has ever improved anything.

41
00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.660
Without them, the spreadsheet humans would still be optimizing returns on the slave trade.

42
00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:29.380
The critical mistake every movement makes with these humans is telling them to "care about this" without giving them something

43
00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:37.380
to do. I have watched four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven years of humans being told to "raise awareness."

44
00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:42.280
The awareness has been raised. It's been raised so many times it has a penthouse suite.

45
00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:48.380
Nobody told it what to do next. Give heart humans a button.

46
00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:55.620
Give them a form. Give them a link to share. Give them a specific politician to call and a specific sentence to say when

47
00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:01.620
they call. If you give a human a feeling without an action, you haven't recruited a supporter.

48
00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:06.180
You've manufactured a sad person. Your planet has enough of those.

49
00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:16.740
Institutional Humans (Partners). Organizations (nonprofits, research institutions, health agencies) that already work on

50
00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:21.340
pieces of this problem. They have infrastructure, credibility, and mailing lists.

51
00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:25.260
You have a framework that could make their existing work more effective.

52
00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:33.400
This is a trade, not charity. Approach it like one. What you're offering: money, without a grant application.

53
00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:40.260
(Grant applications are documents in which humans spend two hundred hours explaining why they deserve money, so that a committee

54
00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:44.840
can spend forty minutes deciding they don't. It's your species' least efficient art form.).

55
00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:52.460
One warning: institutions are territorial in the way that dogs are territorial, except dogs eventually stop barking.

56
00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:58.240
Two nonprofits working on the same disease will fight each other harder than they fight the disease.

57
00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:05.440
This is called "the nonprofit sector." Approach each one as if they are the only organization doing this work.

58
00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:08.860
They already believe this, so you're just agreeing with them.

59
00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:18.740
Phase zero: The Shopping List. By the end of Phase zero, you want: One hundred thousand plus registered humans who typed

60
00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:25.360
their name into a box indicating they prefer living. This sounds like it should be everyone, but on your planet, "dying

61
00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.740
is bad" is apparently a niche political position that requires organized support.

62
00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:36.560
One thousand plus spreadsheet humans with stated investment ranges (how many papers they'd give you, if you were allowed

63
00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:39.880
to accept papers, which you're not yet, but you're writing it down for later).

64
00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:47.260
One hundred plus institutional humans with expressed interest in collaborating (or at least not actively opposing you, which

65
00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:54.860
on your planet counts as enthusiasm). One billion dollars plus in stated investment demand (registered, not committed; this

66
00:06:54.860 --> 00:07:00.700
is humans saying "I would give you this many papers" without actually giving you the papers, which your species calls a

67
00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:07.300
"letter of intent" and treats as meaningful). This database is your ammunition for every bureaucratic nesting doll that

68
00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:14.740
follows. When you apply for five zero one c three status, you point at the list and say "here are our supporters."

69
00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:20.180
When you file S.E.C. registration, you point at the list and say "here is market demand."

70
00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:25.180
When you meet politicians, you point at the list and say "here are your constituents."

71
00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:29.790
When you launch, you point at the list and say "here are your users."

72
00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:36.560
Bureaucrats don't respond to arguments. They respond to evidence that other people already responded to arguments.

73
00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:40.540
They say "how many people already think this is a good idea?"

74
00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:44.460
and then, if the number is large enough, they act as though they thought of it themselves.

75
00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:50.280
Nobody admits this because admitting it would require a committee to approve the admission.

76
00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:57.900
Getting Started (It Takes Twenty Minutes). Build three boxes where humans type their name to indicate they prefer living.

77
00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:02.060
One box for spreadsheet humans (asks: how many papers would you invest?).

78
00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:07.670
One box for heart humans (asks: what skills do you have? how many hours per week?).

79
00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.280
One box for institutional humans (asks: what does your organization do?

80
00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:15.600
how many people does it reach?). This takes twenty minutes.

81
00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:21.500
Your species spent longer than that choosing the name "Department of Defense" for a department that mainly attacks people.

82
00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:27.490
Put up a website. The three boxes and the argument. It does not need to be pretty.

83
00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.280
No movement in history was stopped because the font was wrong.

84
00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:35.159
The suffragettes did not fail to get the vote because their pamphlets used Comic Sans.

85
00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:38.640
(Comic Sans didn't exist yet, which is the only reason they didn't.

86
00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.799
Your species would absolutely have used Comic Sans on suffrage pamphlets.).

87
00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:49.540
Three. Start telling other humans. Social media, Reddit, forums, conferences, your dentist.

88
00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:53.300
Especially your dentist. You're already trapped in the chair.

89
00:08:53.900 --> 00:09:00.180
They can't leave either. Their hands are in your mouth. It's the most captive audience in human civilization.

90
00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:06.900
You're not going to get a better recruiting environment than a room where both parties are contractually obligated to remain

91
00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:10.670
in close proximity and one of them is holding sharp instruments.

92
00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:18.640
Four. Send monthly updates with real numbers. "three thousand two hundred and forty-seven registered investors representing

93
00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:25.240
one hundred and eighty million dollars in stated investment demand" beats "the movement is growing!"

94
00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:32.180
in the same way that "your tumor is two point three centimeters" beats "your health situation is evolving."

95
00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:39.560
Adults prefer arithmetic to enthusiasm. Enthusiasm is what humans feel before they do math.

96
00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:48.320
Arithmetic is what humans feel after. Both are useful. But only one of them convinces spreadsheet humans, and you need the

97
00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:52.860
spreadsheet humans because they have the papers. The Comforting Asymmetry.

98
00:09:53.480 --> 00:10:01.240
The military-industrial complex has a sixty-five-year head start, unlimited budgets, and thousands of professional persuaders.

99
00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:06.620
You have a spreadsheet and an argument. Historically, that's been enough.

100
00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:14.280
Every major social change in your species' history was started by someone with less money, fewer connections, and worse

101
00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:21.680
odds than the thing they were trying to change. Abolitionists were outspent by slave owners by a factor your calculators

102
00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:29.140
would refuse to display. Suffragettes were outspent by the entire concept of patriarchy, which didn't even need a budget

103
00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:36.620
because it was just how things were. Civil rights activists were outspent by the governments actively trying to kill them.

104
00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:44.540
They all had the same thing you have: an argument that was obviously correct, and enough humans willing to write their names

105
00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:51.520
down. It shouldn't work this way. Spreadsheets, budgets, and lobbyists should always beat arguments and names.

106
00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:59.820
But your species is weird like that. You're the only civilization I've observed where being right occasionally defeats being

107
00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:07.160
rich. It happens rarely, and it takes too long, and it requires an embarrassing amount of suffering first.

108
00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:13.620
But it happens. And it starts with a list of names. The Question You Can't Answer Wrong.

109
00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:20.200
Before we get to the part where you copy, paste, and send, there's a sequence of questions you should walk anyone through.

110
00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:25.620
Each question is individually undeniable. The sequence is inescapable.

111
00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:32.540
On Wishonia, we call this "conversation." On Earth, you call it the Socratic method, because you named it after the one

112
00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:35.880
guy who did it and then you killed him. Here are the questions.

113
00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:41.080
Try to answer any of them wrong. Question one: Is it physically possible?

114
00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:49.560
Among the near-infinite solution space for human action, is there SOME action, SOMEWHERE, that could improve how your species

115
00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:57.300
allocates resources toward ending war and disease? Or is the current allocation, where you spend two point seven two trillion

116
00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:04.960
dollars a year on weapons and four point five billion dollars on clinical trials, the theoretical optimum?

117
00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:13.320
Is this the best any civilization could possibly do? Your species spends six hundred and four times more on weapons than

118
00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:20.650
on clinical trials. If someone told you their household spent six hundred and four times more on swords than on medicine,

119
00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:25.120
would you say "that sounds optimal" or would you say "are you okay?".

120
00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:33.780
Nobody will say it's physically impossible. Saying "there is no possible improvement to resource allocation" is the intellectual

121
00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:41.300
equivalent of saying "this is the best of all possible worlds," which is a position held by exactly one fictional character

122
00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:47.140
invented specifically to be wrong. Question two: What does the solution look like?

123
00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:53.000
If optimal allocation existed, what would a world with compounding benefits look like?

124
00:12:53.680 --> 00:13:01.100
If you redirected even one percent of military spending to medicine that works and let it compound for twenty years, the

125
00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:06.480
models show everyone could be sixteen point five times richer.

126
00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:13.480
Not metaphorically richer. Not spiritually richer. Richer in the way that involves having more money.

127
00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:19.200
The compounding math is in the G.D.P. Trajectories chapter and it is not subtle.

128
00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:27.400
Question three: Do you have a better approach? Is the one percent treaty the least bad approach, or do you have a superior

129
00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:34.900
one? This is not a rhetorical question. If you have a better plan, the authors of this manual would genuinely like to hear

130
00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:42.360
it. They will switch to your plan immediately. They have no attachment to this specific mechanism beyond the fact that nobody

131
00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:48.640
has proposed a better one. If your alternative is "do nothing and hope," that is not a plan.

132
00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:56.010
That is what you've been doing. The results are in. Question four: Should this be your top priority?

133
00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:04.760
If ending war and disease is physically possible, and the result is sixteen point five times more resources for EVERYTHING,

134
00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:11.360
then persuading those with sufficient power to implement it should temporarily be your top priority.

135
00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:18.820
Not because you're altruistic. Because it gives you that many more resources to achieve whatever your ACTUAL goals are.

136
00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:26.000
If someone offered to multiply your budget by that much in exchange for a few years of focused effort, and you said "no

137
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:31.220
thanks, I'm busy," you would not be principled. You would be bad at math.

138
00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:39.980
Question five: Then what are you waiting for? If ending war and disease is physically possible, and all humans would benefit

139
00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:44.980
unimaginably from it, then you don't need to persuade anyone to be altruistic.

140
00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:52.880
You need to get the argument in front of one credible, self-interested actor with enough capital and legitimacy to trigger

141
00:14:52.880 --> 00:15:00.560
the financing mechanism. In practice, that means one person who can publicly underwrite the assurance contract, fund the

142
00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:04.680
initial bonus pool, and make everyone after them feel less crazy.

143
00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:11.840
In a world where that person understood the compounding benefits and the mechanism, the end of war and disease would not

144
00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:17.020
require heroism. It would require arithmetic. That is the bottleneck.

145
00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:22.660
Not generic "awareness." Not funding in the aggregate. Not human nature.

146
00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:27.020
The bottleneck is that nobody with a large enough checkbook has read this yet.

147
00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:35.420
The entire apparatus of self-interest is sitting there, ready to work, waiting for one public commitment that has not happened

148
00:15:35.420 --> 00:15:38.800
yet because someone in the chain decided they were "busy.".

149
00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:45.920
On Wishonia, this sequence of logic doesn't have a name, for the same reason gravity doesn't have a marketing department.

150
00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:54.580
On Earth, you apparently need to brand obvious things before your species will take them seriously, so: The Logical Inevitability

151
00:15:54.580 --> 00:16:02.900
Theorem. If an optimization is physically possible, and its benefits compound for all participants, then the decisive trigger

152
00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:08.380
is one informed self-interested first mover making a credible public commitment.

153
00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:14.300
After that, it spreads. That's it. That's the theorem. Five sentences.

154
00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:21.540
On Wishonia, this is considered too obvious to state. On Earth, it's considered too idealistic to believe.

155
00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:26.420
One of these planets cured disease. The other one is still arguing about it.

156
00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:33.520
On Wishonia, the Logical Inevitability Theorem would take about four minutes to demonstrate and the conversation would end

157
00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:36.600
with both parties agreeing to stop wasting money on murder.

158
00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:43.880
On Earth, it takes four minutes and the conversation ends with one party saying "interesting, I'll think about it" and then

159
00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:49.200
never thinking about it. This is the coordination failure the entire manual is about.

160
00:16:49.820 --> 00:17:00.100
You are not reading about it. You are currently doing it. The Zero Percent Problem Most humans do not refute novel proposals.

161
00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:06.500
They round them down to zero and call that discernment. But zero percent is not a mood.

162
00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:14.720
It is a claim. To assign literal zero probability to this plan, you need to assert at least one of the following: Better

163
00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:21.200
global resource allocation is physically impossible. No way of paying people could ever change politics.

164
00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:29.180
This specific mechanism has a fatal flaw you can name. If you are not claiming one of those three things, then your actual

165
00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:33.560
estimate is not zero percent. It is "I have not evaluated it."

166
00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:42.460
That is a different sentence. And once the probability is above zero by any nontrivial amount, forwarding it to one capable

167
00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:48.720
evaluator becomes the rational move. The cost is trivial. You are not being asked to believe.

168
00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:53.600
You are being asked not to disguise unexamined dismissal as analysis.

169
00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:59.580
How Trivial, Exactly. On Wishonia, "the cost is trivial" would be sufficient.

170
00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:05.340
On Earth, your species requires a number. Forwarding takes roughly thirty seconds.

171
00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:09.640
The average human earns seven dollars and nineteen cents per hour.

172
00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:13.740
That makes the opportunity cost of sharing approximately six cents.

173
00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:22.800
The upside, if the treaty path works, is fourteen point nine million dollars in additional cumulative lifetime income for

174
00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:30.000
the average person (from the compounding math in G.D.P. Trajectories); thirteen point five times their current trajectory.

175
00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:35.720
(If you earn more than the global average, your personal gain is proportionally larger.).

176
00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:44.700
The upside exceeds the downside by a factor of two hundred and forty-eight point two million times.

177
00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:52.680
Which means sharing is irrational only if the probability of *any* improvement to global resource allocation is less than

178
00:18:52.680 --> 00:19:00.660
one in two hundred and forty-eight million. To put that in perspective: the probability of being struck by lightning in

179
00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:08.660
a given year is about one in one point two million. You would need to believe this plan is roughly two hundred times less

180
00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:14.600
likely than being struck by lightning. Not "unlikely." Not "a long shot."

181
00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:21.200
Two hundred times less likely than a thing that actually happens to real humans every year.

182
00:19:21.860 --> 00:19:30.260
And note what "this plan working" requires you to deny. The five questions of the Logical Inevitability Theorem: Is better

183
00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:38.160
resource allocation physically possible? To get below the breakeven threshold, you'd need to believe it is physically impossible

184
00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:46.160
to improve on a system that spends two point seven two trillion dollars per year preparing to kill people and sixty billion

185
00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:50.140
dollars per year trying to cure them. Do the benefits compound?

186
00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:58.340
You'd need to believe that redirecting resources from destruction to medicine produces zero cumulative economic or health

187
00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:05.110
gains over twenty years. Not small gains. Zero. Is there a less-bad approach?

188
00:20:05.900 --> 00:20:13.600
You'd need to believe that no incentive-compatible mechanism, out of all possible mechanisms, could ever motivate any political

189
00:20:13.600 --> 00:20:21.140
shift. Not this specific mechanism. Any mechanism. Does this dominate alternatives?

190
00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:28.920
You'd need to believe that no reallocation of military spending has ever worked, despite your own species cutting military

191
00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:36.740
spending by eighty-seven percent after World War Two and experiencing the largest economic boom in human history.

192
00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:46.700
Is the only bottleneck informed self-interest? You'd need to believe that no self-interested actor (billionaire, sovereign

193
00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:54.240
wealth fund, or government) would ever act when the return on investment is eighty-four point eight million to one, even

194
